def table(rows,widths):
 t=Table([[Paragraph(str(c),cell) for c in r] for r in rows],colWidths=widths,hAlign='LEFT')
 t.setStyle(TableStyle([('VALIGN',(0,0),(-1,-1),'TOP'),('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.HexColor('#e9eff3')),('LINEBELOW',(0,0),(-1,0),.6,colors.HexColor('#657787')),('LINEBELOW',(0,-1),(-1,-1),.4,colors.grey),('LEFTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),5),('RIGHTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),5),('TOPPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),5),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),5)]))
 story.extend([t,Spacer(1,8)]);source.append(str(rows))
def page():story.append(PageBreak());source.append('\n--- PAGE ---\n')
class Chart(Flowable):
 def __init__(self,kind):Flowable.__init__(self);self.width=CW;self.height=200;self.kind=kind
 def draw(self):
  c=self.canv;c.setFont('Times-Roman',10)
  if self.kind=='bench':
   labels=['Key / 2 workers / 8 parts','Sedona / 1 worker / 8 parts','Sedona / 2 workers / 8 parts','Sedona / 2 workers / 32 parts'];vals=[4.179,7.876,7.426,8.990]
   x0=166;scale=(CW-x0-40)/10
   for i,(label,v) in enumerate(zip(labels,vals)):
    y=164-i*35;c.setFillColor(colors.black);c.drawRightString(x0-8,y+5,label);c.setFillColor(colors.HexColor('#35647f'));c.rect(x0,y,v*scale,19,fill=1,stroke=0);c.setFillColor(colors.black);c.drawString(x0+v*scale+5,y+5,f'{v:.3f}')
   c.drawString(x0,12,'Median elapsed time (seconds; lower is better)')
  else:
   data=list(csv.DictReader((ROOT/'analysis/data/week8_handoff/concentration.csv').open()))
   groups=[('Urban centres',{30},'#285f82'),('Urban clusters',{21,22,23},'#b36b2c'),('Rural classes',{11,12,13},'#4b8059')]
   x0=38;y0=40;pw=CW-50;ph=125
   for tick in [10,20,30,40,50]:
    y=y0+(tick-10)/40*ph;c.setStrokeColor(colors.HexColor('#d6dce0'));c.line(x0,y,x0+pw,y);c.setFillColor(colors.black);c.drawRightString(x0-6,y-3,str(tick))
   for year in [1975,1985,1995,2005,2020]:c.drawCentredString(x0+(year-1975)/45*pw,24,str(year))
   for i,(label,codes,color) in enumerate(groups):
    c.setStrokeColor(colors.HexColor(color));c.setLineWidth(1.7);pts=[]
    for year in range(1975,2021,5):
     v=sum(float(r['population_share'])*100 for r in data if int(r['year'])==year and int(r['settlement_class']) in codes);pts.append((x0+(year-1975)/45*pw,y0+(v-10)/40*ph))
    for a,b in zip(pts,pts[1:]):c.line(*a,*b)
    c.setFillColor(colors.HexColor(color))
    for x,y in pts:c.circle(x,y,2,fill=1,stroke=0)
    x=10+i*145;c.line(x,191,x+14,191);c.setFillColor(colors.black);c.drawString(x+18,188,label)
   c.drawString(x0,5,'Population share (%) on the fixed common footprint')

h("India's Urban Concentration and Emerging Growth Centres using GHSL")
p('<b>Final Project Report | Data Engineering at Scale | Team Vector</b>',caption)
p('Himanshu Arora (ahimanshu@iisc.ac.in)<br/>[Author 2 name] ([Author 2 email])<br/>[Author 3 name] ([Author 3 email])<br/>[Author 4 name] ([Author 4 email])',caption)
h('Abstract')
p('This project builds and evaluates a reproducible geospatial data pipeline for examining population concentration, built-up expansion and settlement change in India using GHSL. It integrates native 100 m population and built-surface rasters with matching-year 1 km settlement classes, retaining both native records and aligned coarse summaries. Apache Spark supports validation, joins, aggregation and SQL analysis; Apache Sedona supplies executed spatial joins, and a pilot demonstrates real single-node HDFS storage. The accepted execution covers 13 tiles and 156 tile-year jobs across 1975-2030. Historical analysis uses 1975-2020 only, with a fixed common footprint of 3,280,884 coarse cells. Regional raw-data checks and analytical consistency checks passed. Repeated pilot benchmarks quantify latency, throughput, memory and partition sensitivity. Results show rising population shares in urban classes and generate exploratory growth and absorption candidates. Coverage is incomplete: tile R8_C27 is excluded, and candidate events are not independently confirmed settlements. [1-6]')
h('1. Problem: definition and motivation')
p('The original proposal asks how population and built-up development became concentrated in large urban centres between 1975 and 2020, which smaller settlements became connected to expanding metropolitan regions, and which developed independently. Answering these questions requires consistent measurements across time, products and spatial resolutions. A misaligned raster join can assign population to the wrong settlement category; inconsistent missing-data treatment can create artificial growth. The engineering problem is therefore as important as the final map. [1]')
p('The motivation is to distinguish concentration in existing urban systems from the emergence of other growth centres. Population measures estimated residential presence, built surface measures physical development, and settlement classes provide a common categorical description. Their combined trajectories support descriptive comparison; they do not establish migration mechanisms, economic integration or causal effects.')
h('1.1 Design goals and required features')
p('The required system must preserve the common grid, distinguish zero from missing values, integrate all three GHSL products, process ten historical epochs plus two projection tests, and produce reusable partitioned Parquet. Analytical features are settlement transition matrices, urban-class concentration shares, fixed-district summaries, and candidate metropolitan absorption and independent growth. Reproducibility requires source provenance, saved configurations, explicit validation gates and restartable processing. The professor feedback additionally requires an executed geospatial Spark engine and quantitative performance evaluation. [1-3]')
page()
h('1.2 Scope and measurable engineering goals')
p('The work was delivered in three stages: weeks 1-3 established all-product input validation, HDFS zones and Sedona spatial checks; weeks 4-5 produced reusable Spark/Sedona integration and repeated end-to-end benchmarks; weeks 6-8 extended execution to the accepted multi-tile footprint and produced temporal, district and candidate analyses. The final scope contains 624 source archives: four raster components for 13 tiles and 12 epochs. [2-4]')
p('The original plan contained 168 tile-years. Fresh-source retries for R8_C27 still produced no valid analytical cells in the selected country portion. With explicit approval, the final study excludes this tile in every epoch and accepts 156 jobs. Excluded geography remains missing, never zero-filled or extrapolated. Thus this report describes the verified study footprint, not complete India coverage. [4]')
p('Predeclared pilot engineering thresholds were median latency at most 120 seconds, throughput at least 3,500 cells/s and peak sampled process-tree RSS at most 8 GB. Correctness remains a hard gate regardless of speed. Scalability is evaluated through input-size variation, local worker counts and partition sensitivity. These tests assess a single-machine implementation; they do not demonstrate multi-node scaling. [2,3]')
h('2. Approach and methods: sources and data model')
table([['Source / component','Native grid and role'],['GHS-POP R2023A V1_0','100 m estimated residential population counts'],['GHS-BUILT-S R2023A V1_0','100 m total built surface and non-residential built surface; square metres'],['GHS-SMOD R2023A V2_0','1 km settlement category, matched by epoch'],['geoBoundaries gbOpen IND','Country geometry represented year 2014; fixed 2021 district geometry, 735 features']],[CW*.40,CW*.60])
p('The nominal epochs are 1975, 1980, 1985, 1990, 1995, 2000, 2005, 2010, 2015, 2020, 2025 and 2030. The last two are labelled projection tests and are excluded from historical conclusions. Official JRC archive downloads replace the proposed Earth Engine export path; Earth Engine execution is not claimed for this delivered pipeline. Boundary vintages are fixed reproducible study geometries, not historical district reconstructions or official Indian administrative boundaries. [2-4]')
p('All layers use the World Mollweide contract (ESRI:54009). The aligned 1 km grid has global upper-left origin x = -18,041,000 m and y = 9,000,000 m. Keys combine grid identity, cell identity and year; coarse cell identifiers use global row and column indices. Grid metadata, origin, resolution, extent and masks are checked before joining. Matching nominal resolution alone is insufficient.')
p('A native record retains population, total built surface, non-residential built surface, the inherited matching-year settlement class, spatial identity and epoch. Coarse records sum the quantitative measures and retain completeness information. Population is a count, so it is summed rather than averaged. A repeated parent settlement class does not become a new independently observed 100 m classification. NoData is null; valid zero remains zero. Partial boundary cells are flagged and not expanded to represent a full square kilometre. [2,3]')
page()
h('2.1 Architecture and processing flow')
table([['Stage','Processing and persisted result'],['1. Acquire and audit','JRC archives -> source manifests, ZIP CRC and SHA256 checks'],['2. Prepare','Windowed raster extraction -> aligned native cells and coarse parent metadata'],['3. Integrate','Spark product joins / Sedona pilot joins -> validated native and 1 km Parquet'],['4. Analyse','Historical common mask -> transitions, concentration, districts and candidate events'],['5. Verify and publish','Independent comparisons, checked copies and stage markers -> reports, tables and handoff ZIP']],[CW*.28,CW*.72])
p('Raw, cleaned and curated HDFS zones were actually exercised in the pilot. The full accepted multi-tile outputs use local partitioned Parquet to avoid duplicate national storage. Spark runs locally, with two workers in the multi-tile execution. This design permits bounded processing and restart at tile-year boundaries, but the machine and storage remain local bottlenecks. The compact handoff contains source, results and evidence; the large native datasets and runtimes remain outside it. [2-4]')
p('Spark DataFrames validate schemas, ranges, epoch membership, unique keys and coverage, join native products, and aggregate values. Spark SQL produces class summaries, temporal transitions and district summaries. Parquet is partitioned by epoch type and year. Unique-key checks precede exact null-safe comparisons of persisted output, so duplicate rows cannot hide behind aggregate equality.')
h('2.2 Executed geospatial Spark operations')
p('Apache Sedona 1.9.1 ran with Spark 3.5.6, Scala 2.12.18 and Java 17.0.7. Phase 1 used ST_Point, ST_Covers, ST_PolygonFromEnvelope and ST_Area for country coverage, district assignment and native-cell containment within coarse parents. Every one of 434,363 pilot cells agreed with an independent Shapely district reference. Phase 2 used an indexed point-to-polygon join assigning native centres to matching-year SMOD parents. Saved physical plans are required to contain a Sedona spatial operator; this establishes functional use rather than installation alone. [2,3]')
p('For aligned regular grids, a key-based join is a useful baseline. Sedona adds geometric containment semantics and a separately checked path. The larger run uses its validated grid integration and raster-centre district attribution; it is not represented as a national Sedona benchmark. All district summaries preserve UNASSIGNED and AMBIGUOUS labels to make unmatched or overlapping coverage visible.')
h('2.3 Reliability, recovery and scientific safeguards')
p('A preserved failed attempt exposed a zero-byte Parquet chunk. The revised writer validates temporary outputs and every Parquet footer, copies files with SHA256 verification, and atomically publishes completed directories. Resume verifies completed checkpoints and implementation identity. Failed attempts and migrations are retained. Transport retries are bounded; data-validity failures are not silently retried or bypassed. [4]')
p('The accepted-coverage view links to preserved verified pair outputs and records excluded jobs explicitly. Downstream stages require successful inputs and propagate the coverage disclosure. This prevents a partial result from being mistaken for a complete national run. Recovery required engineering intervention, so the original objective of completing the run without manual correction was not achieved. [4,6]')
page()
h('2.4 Temporal analysis and candidate identification')
p('Historical comparisons retain only coarse cells complete across all ten historical epochs. The common mask contains 3,280,884 cells; 13,743 additional cells are excluded for temporal completeness. Ten periods comprise the nine adjacent five-year intervals and the 1975-2020 comparison. Settlement transitions are counted by origin and destination class. Population and built-surface changes are computed on the same cells. Per-epoch totals and common-mask temporal totals have different denominators and should not be compared as if interchangeable. [4]')
p('The configured rural classes are 11, 12 and 13; urban classes are 21, 22, 23 and 30, with class 30 denoting urban centres. Water code 10 is handled separately. Connected-component overlap and lineage rules generate exploratory absorption and independent-growth events. The baseline uses eight-neighbour connectivity, minimum retained fraction 0.5, minimum growth fraction 0.1 and minimum baseline size two cells. Components near missing-data or country edges are censored, while ambiguous lineage and below-minimum cases remain explicit statuses. [4]')
p('This is a deterministic spatial and rule-based method. No machine-learning model is trained or evaluated. Supervised classification would require independent labelled examples, which are unavailable here. Spatial adjacency and class change indicate candidates for review; they do not establish administrative absorption, functional metropolitan integration or unique town identities.')
h('3. Evaluation: experiment design and correctness')
table([['Validation level','Executed evidence / observed result'],['Inputs and geometry','All 48 pilot raster components audited; grid, mask, category and range checks passed'],['Pilot storage and integration','HDFS retrieval verified; 5,212,356 native and 53,460 coarse records; zero rejected records or incomplete pilot trajectories'],['Independent spatial reference','434,363 pilot assignments matched Shapely; no missing, duplicate or mismatched assignments'],['Raw regional comparison','750 coarse cell-years, three regions, ten historical epochs; zero numerical differences and exact class agreement'],['Analytical follow-up','26,870 regional cell-period checks and 108,804 candidate-rule checks; no mismatches'],['Delivery checks','Six final stages passed; final ZIP checks verify CRC, hashes, membership, safe paths, syntax and required evidence']],[CW*.33,CW*.67])
p('Independent Arrow checks compare every native, coarse and SQL-summary column in the pilot. Aggregate reference comparisons use relative tolerance 1e-10 and absolute tolerance 1e-5; Spark output rereads use exact null-safe comparisons. District counts and values conserve the complete-case totals. These checks establish numerical consistency of processing, rather than the real-world truth of each source estimate. [2-5]')
p('The raw regional evaluation covers Delhi, Mumbai and Bengaluru. The initial Mumbai window contained no retained country-mask cells; its centre was moved 8.06 km using a deterministic coverage-only rule, without reference to comparison outcomes. Delhi and Bengaluru were unchanged. This adjustment is disclosed because regional samples are sensitive to mask coverage. Arithmetic and candidate-rule agreement do not supply external detection precision or recall. [4,5]')
page()
h('3.1 Quantitative performance and scalability')
p('The benchmark machine had four physical / eight logical CPUs and approximately 17.2 GB RAM, running macOS. Phase 1 tested deterministic spatial subsets of 10,000, 100,000 and 434,363 cells, with eight partitions, one warmup and five measured repetitions for each worker count. At the largest size, spatial-join median time fell from 2.858 s with one worker to 1.514 s with two, a 1.888x time ratio. This cached-input, materialized-join experiment does not include the entire pipeline. [2]')
p('Phase 2 measured fresh reads, all-product joins, aggregation and writing of native, coarse and summary outputs for one real 2020 epoch of 434,363 cells. Each scenario used one warmup and three measured repetitions; all 16 trials passed persisted-result comparisons. Startup and post-write correctness checks are outside the timer. DataFrames are not reused; OS cache is uncontrolled. [3]')
story.append(Chart('bench'))
p('<b>Figure 1.</b> End-to-end pilot latency from saved phase-2 medians. Each scenario processes the same epoch and products; bars are single-machine results. [3]',caption)
table([['Scenario','P95 (s)','Cells/s','Peak RSS (GB)'],['Key, 2 workers, 8 partitions','4.900','103,945','2.218'],['Sedona, 1 worker, 8 partitions','8.401','55,152','2.235'],['Sedona, 2 workers, 8 partitions','8.309','58,489','2.306'],['Sedona, 2 workers, 32 partitions','9.551','48,315','2.574']],[CW*.49,CW*.16,CW*.17,CW*.18])
p('All scenarios met the three declared thresholds. Sedona one-to-two-worker speedup was only 1.061x end to end; at two workers and eight partitions it took 1.777x the key-baseline median. Increasing to 32 partitions increased median latency by 21.1%, consistent with overhead dominating this bounded workload. These results favour selecting join strategy and partition count from measured workload behaviour rather than assuming more parallelism is always faster.')
p('Saved metrics also include CPU, task time, garbage collection, shuffle/spill and output size. RSS sampling every 100 ms can miss short peaks and double-count shared pages. P95 from three or five repetitions is descriptive. The 156 accepted tile-year timings total 117,739.66 s and imply 33,361 analytical rows/s in aggregate, including Spark startup, validation and writes but excluding preceding acquisition and preparation. These are single observations, not total project elapsed time or repeated cluster scaling. [2-4]')
page()
h('3.2 Feature results: concentration and regional change')
story.append(Chart('concentration'))
p('<b>Figure 2.</b> Population shares by grouped settlement class, 1975-2020, using saved concentration.csv values on the fixed common complete footprint. Category membership can change across epochs. [4]',caption)
p('Urban-centre population share increased from 29.4% to 38.7%, while urban-cluster share rose from 35.5% to 44.3%. Rural-class share fell from 35.1% to 16.9%. Urban-cluster built-surface share increased from 26.9% to 47.2%. These descriptive changes indicate greater concentration in urban classes within the observed footprint; they cannot be read as full-India estimates or as migration alone, because cells can change category. [4]')
table([['Study window','Common cells','Rural-to-urban cells','Population growth','Built growth'],['Delhi','961','194','299.2%','42.0%'],['Mumbai vicinity*','765','142','162.1%','124.9%'],['Bengaluru','961','428','368.3%','267.8%']],[CW*.28,CW*.16,CW*.20,CW*.18,CW*.18])
p('<b>Table 1.</b> Changes from 1975 to 2020 in 31 x 31 km study squares. Each percentage uses its own measure\'s 1975 baseline. *Mumbai uses the coverage-adjusted centre. These squares are not administrative city boundaries. [5]',caption)
p('Bengaluru shows the largest proportional population and built-surface increases among these windows and the most rural-to-urban cells. In all three windows, population grew proportionally faster than built surface. This supports comparison of demographic and horizontal physical change, but does not measure building height or demonstrate a causal mechanism. Different initial densities, masks and baselines limit cross-region interpretation.')
h('3.3 Candidate events and sensitivity')
p('Baseline screening produced 108,804 overlapping period/component events: 23,317 independent-growth candidates, 5,085 metropolitan-absorption candidates, 9,317 ambiguous-lineage cases, 8,336 boundary-censored cases, 3,606 below-minimum cases and 59,143 no-candidate events. Events overlap across periods and must not be reported as distinct towns. Review GeoJSON uses representative cells rather than settlement boundaries. [4]')
p('Four-neighbour connectivity produced 26,010 independent-growth and 12,103 absorption candidates. Raising the growth threshold to 0.25 with eight-neighbour connectivity reduced independent-growth candidates to 14,577. Connectivity changes the component universe; counts are not accuracy scores. Threshold sensitivity and missing independent labels require cautious interpretation. [4]')
page()
h('4. Summary: achievement against the proposal')
p('The project delivered a reusable all-product integration pipeline, executed Sedona spatial operations, real pilot HDFS storage, repeated performance evaluation, ten historical epochs plus two projection tests, and the required transition, district and candidate outputs. All six final processing stages passed for the accepted footprint. Correctness was evaluated through source and grid audits, independent references, conservation, persisted-output comparisons and sampled raw-data checks. The strongest contribution is the combination of explicit geospatial semantics, repeatable data engineering and transparent validation; no new classification algorithm is claimed. [2-6]')
p('The stated pilot latency, throughput and sampled-memory goals were achieved. The original complete-India and uninterrupted-run objectives were not fully achieved: R8_C27 was excluded after failed fresh-source retries, and engineering recovery was necessary. JRC downloads replaced Earth Engine exports; national outputs use local Parquet rather than full national HDFS storage. Multi-machine scalability and external confirmation of settlement candidates remain untested. These are limitations of the delivered result, not successful implementations of the original objectives. [6]')
h('4.1 Impact and future extensions')
p('The outputs support reproducible screening of long-term settlement transitions and the identification of cases for closer urban analysis. Explicit common masks, coverage flags and fixed district vintages reduce avoidable comparisons across incompatible data. The resulting tables and review points are useful research inputs, but should not determine administrative or planning decisions without independent validation.')
p('Future work should first investigate the excluded tile and boundary/source discrepancy, then re-evaluate coverage without changing missing values to zero. A labelled sample from independent settlement and administrative evidence would permit precision/recall evaluation of candidate detection. Further extensions include temporal component tracking with unique identities, historical boundary harmonisation, sensitivity to masks and thresholds, and replicated multi-node benchmarks that measure end-to-end I/O, partition skew, fault recovery and cost. These extensions require new execution or reference evidence.')
h('4.2 Reproducibility and deliverables')
p('The reviewed handoff is GHSL_Weeks1_8_Accepted_Coverage_Final_v2.zip. The submission guide explains source dependencies, environment pins and verification without rerunning the pipeline. Its 192 files passed eight archive checks, and the 15 November recheck matched the original checksum. Native raster/Parquet data and runtimes remain outside the compact archive. Source repository: github.com/himarora1/ghsl-india-urban-growth. Repository access must meet the course submission requirement separately; the recorded publication was private.')
h('References and evidence')
p('[1] Team Vector. <i>Analysis of India\'s Urban Concentration and Emerging Growth Centres using GHSL Dataset</i>. Original project proposal, pp. 1-2.<br/>[2] Project execution evidence. <i>Phase 1 Completion Report - Weeks 1-3</i>, 7 November 2026; phase1/reports/PHASE1_COMPLETION_REPORT.md.<br/>[3] Project execution evidence. <i>Phase 2 Completion Report - Weeks 4-5</i>, 7 November 2026; phase2/reports/PHASE2_COMPLETION_REPORT.md.<br/>[4] Final handoff report and associated validation/tables, 10 November 2026; analysis/data/week8_handoff/REPORT.md; national_candidates/validation.json.<br/>[5] <i>Regional Analytical Validation and Interpretation</i>, 10 November 2026; phase3/reports/analytical_review/REPORT.md and validation.json.<br/>[6] <i>Final Requirement-to-Evidence Review</i>; phase3/reports/REQUIREMENTS_REVIEW.md; FINAL_ACCEPTANCE.json. Sources underlying [2-5]: European Commission Joint Research Centre GHSL R2023A POP/BUILT-S V1_0 and SMOD V2_0; geoBoundaries gbOpen (Pathways Data Pvt. Ltd. and lgdirectory.gov.in), ODbL 1.0. Apache Sedona 1.9.1 spatial SQL and Apache Spark 3.5.6.',caption)

